XX
XX
CC   RepeatMasker Annotations:
CC        Type: LTR?
CC        SubType: 
CC        Species: Sauria
CC        SearchStages: 
CC        BufferStages:
CC        Description: Derived from the turtle genome and the reconstructed
CC                     ancestor of turtles and archosaurs. TG...CA
CC                     termini and a clear presence of subfamilies
CC                     suggests it to be an LTR, though no constant
CC                     target site duplication is apparent. The
CC                     first 548 bp of a related element active
CC                     in the common ancestor of mammals is represented
CC                     by UCON103. Ancestral to turtles and archosaurs,
CC                     assumed but not not checked to predate the
CC                     split from lepidosaurs (lizards etc.) Absent
CC                     from mammals.
//
ID   AmnHarb1     repeatmasker; DNA;  ???;  1447 BP.
CC   AmnHarb1 DNA
XX
DE   RepbaseID: 
XX
KW   DNA/PIF-Harbinger.
XX
XX
CC   RepeatMasker Annotations:
CC        Type: DNA
CC        SubType: PIF-Harbinger
CC        Species: Amniota
CC        SearchStages: 50
CC        BufferStages:
CC        Description: 21 bp TIRs, currently not matching other
CC                     DNA transposons. Translated matches to Harbinger
CC                     nuclease/transposase are apparent from pos
CC                     464 to 943 (e.g. to AA 33-219 of the 369
CC                     AA Harbinger transposase XP_006011388.1 in
CC                     Latimeria chalumnae). It is unclear if the
CC                     frame shifts and stopcodons are imperfections
CC                     of the consensus; most copies could be from
CC                     a non-autonomous source gene. Mostly reconstructed
CC                     from the ancestral genome of archosaurs and
CC                     turtles. Fragment of the 1447 bp consensus
CC                     were earlier represented by UCON 92 (pos
CC                     344-214, 86%), UCON84 (621-811, 85%) and
CC                     UCON90 (944-808, 90%).
//
